/// \defgroup grp_w1012_wwlib_mpmath_encode_prime WWLib big-number encoding, random numbers and primality tests
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief The XMP_ routines in mpmath.cpp that turn big numbers into
///        bytes, DER or text and back, draw random big numbers from a
///        Straw, and test numbers for primality.
///
/// These routines sit around the arithmetic core described in
/// \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath, which also explains the number format: an
/// array of 32-bit digits, least significant first, in two's complement,
/// with the digit count passed as the last argument. Their documentation is
/// on the \ref mpmath.cpp file page, next to the other XMP_ routines.
///
/// - Byte strings and DER: XMP_Encode(), XMP_Encode_Bounded(),
///   XMP_Signed_Decode(), XMP_Unsigned_Decode(), XMP_DER_Encode(),
///   XMP_DER_Decode() and XMP_DER_Length_Encode(). The DER routines are
///   how PKey saves and loads its modulus and exponent, which INIClass
///   keeps in a Westwood %INI file as Base64 text.
/// - Text: XMP_Decode_ASCII() parses decimal, hexadecimal, octal or
///   binary text with a radix suffix.
/// - Random numbers: XMP_Randomize() and XMP_Randomize_Bounded() read
///   random bytes from a Straw. Generate_Prime() uses the bounded form to
///   pick its starting candidates.
/// - Primality: XMP_Is_Prime(), built on XMP_Is_Small_Prime(),
///   XMP_Small_Divisors_Test() and XMP_Fermat_Test(), accepts or rejects
///   the candidates that Generate_Prime() tests. XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test()
///   is also in mpmath.cpp; the retail quirks listed on the file page
///   cover it.
///
/// Like the rest of this code, nothing here is driven by game data, and no
/// BFME 2 game code is known to reach it (see
/// \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The byte-level routines read digit arrays as raw little-endian bytes;
///   see XMP_Encode().
/// - Several of them size stack scratch numbers or buffers from
///   MAX_UNIT_PRECISION, so they support at most 64 digits (2,048 bits).
/// - Several bodies have unchecked buffer limits that a port should fix or
///   guard: XMP_Encode_Bounded() with a short output, XMP_Signed_Decode()
///   and XMP_Unsigned_Decode() with too many input bytes, and
///   XMP_Randomize() at full width.

/// \fn int XMP_DER_Length_Encode(unsigned long length, unsigned char *output)
/// \brief Writes the length field of a DER element and returns how many
///        bytes it took.
///
/// DER (the Distinguished Encoding Rules of ASN.1) puts the length of an
/// element's contents in front of them. A length up to 127 takes one byte
/// that holds the length itself (the short form). A longer length takes a
/// first byte of 0x80 plus the number of length bytes that follow, then
/// the length's significant bytes, most significant first (the long form).
/// XMP_DER_Encode is the only known retail caller.
///
/// \param length Length of the contents in bytes.
/// \param output [out] Receives the length field, one to five bytes. Not
///               null.
/// \return Bytes written to output, 1 to 5.
/// \note XMP_DER_Decode reads at most two long-form length bytes, so a
///       field this function writes for a length above 65,535 cannot be
///       read back. A number from Int<64> never needs more than the short
///       form.
/// \see XMP_DER_Encode, XMP_DER_Decode

/// \fn int XMP_DER_Encode(digit const *from, unsigned char *output, int precision)
/// \brief Writes a number as a DER INTEGER: the tag byte, the length, then
///        the shortest big-endian two's-complement form of the number.
///
/// Encodes the number with XMP_Encode into a stack buffer, then writes the
/// INTEGER tag 0x02, the length with XMP_DER_Length_Encode, and the encoded
/// bytes. This is the form in which PKey stores its modulus and exponent.
/// The known retail callers are PKey::Encode_Modulus,
/// PKey::Encode_Exponent, INIClass::Get_PKey and the out-of-line
/// Int::DEREncode of Int<64>.
///
/// \param from      The number.
/// \param output    [out] Receives the element. For Int<64> that is at
///                  most 67 bytes: the tag, a one-byte length and up to 65
///                  bytes of number.
/// \param precision Digit count of from. It is passed on to XMP_Encode,
///                  which reads it as a byte count.
/// \return Bytes written to output.
/// \warning XMP_Encode looks at only the low precision bytes, so of an
///          Int<64> only the low 512 bits are encoded. A wider number is
///          written truncated, and nothing reports it.
/// \see XMP_DER_Decode, XMP_Encode, PKey::Encode_Modulus

/// \fn void XMP_DER_Decode(digit *result, unsigned char const *input, int precision)
/// \brief Reads a DER INTEGER into a number. Input it cannot use is
///        skipped without any report.
///
/// Checks that the first byte is the INTEGER tag 0x02, then reads the
/// length: the short form, or the long form with one or two length bytes.
/// If the number fits in precision digits, XMP_Signed_Decode stores it in
/// result, sign-extended. The known retail callers are the PKey
/// constructor that takes two DER buffers, PKey::Decode_Modulus,
/// PKey::Decode_Exponent and the out-of-line Int::DERDecode of Int<64>.
/// For Int<64> it accepts up to 256 bytes of number, more than
/// XMP_DER_Encode ever writes.
///
/// \param result    [out] The number. Written only when the element is
///                  accepted; it is not cleared first.
/// \param input     The DER element. Not null. Its size is not known
///                  here: once the length is accepted, that many bytes are
///                  read after the length field.
/// \param precision Digit count of result.
/// \note There is no return value. A wrong tag, a long-form length with
///       more than two length bytes, or a number longer than precision * 4
///       bytes leaves result as it was, and the caller cannot tell.
/// \note A first length byte of 0x80, which announces zero length bytes,
///       is read as if one length byte followed.
/// \see XMP_DER_Encode, XMP_Signed_Decode, PKey::Decode_Modulus

/// \fn unsigned XMP_Encode_Bounded(unsigned char *to, unsigned tobytes, digit const *from, int precision)
/// \brief Writes a number into a fixed-size big-endian buffer,
///        sign-extended to fill it.
///
/// Writes exactly tobytes bytes: first copies of the sign byte (0xff for a
/// negative number, 0 otherwise) to pad, then all precision * 4 bytes of
/// the number, most significant first. Unlike XMP_Encode it reads the
/// whole number. int.h wraps it in Int's two-argument Encode member. No
/// retail caller of either is known.
///
/// \param to        [out] Receives tobytes bytes.
/// \param tobytes   Size of the output. Must be at least precision * 4,
///                  which is 256 for Int<64>.
/// \param from      The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of from.
/// \return tobytes.
/// \warning A tobytes smaller than precision * 4 does not truncate. The
///          padding count is an unsigned difference that wraps to a huge
///          value, so the padding loop writes far past the end of to.
///          The copy step after it is written as if truncation were
///          intended, but the loop overruns the buffer first.
/// \see XMP_Encode, XMP_Signed_Decode

/// \fn unsigned XMP_Encode(unsigned char *to, digit const *from, int precision)
/// \brief Writes a number in as few big-endian two's-complement bytes as
///        possible, the contents format of a DER INTEGER.
///
/// Leading bytes that only repeat the sign (0 for a number that is not
/// negative, 0xff for a negative one) are dropped. One sign byte is put
/// back when the top remaining byte's high bit would otherwise give the
/// wrong sign. The rest follow, most significant first, so 0 is written
/// as one 0x00 byte and -1 as one 0xff byte. XMP_DER_Encode is the only
/// known retail caller. int.h also wraps it in Int's one-argument Encode
/// member.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The sign comes from the top digit of the whole number, but the search
/// for the first significant byte starts at byte precision - 1: precision
/// is read as a byte count, not a digit count. Only the low precision bytes
/// are ever written. For Int<64> that is 64 bytes, so a number that needs
/// more than 512 bits loses its upper part. Zero Hour's comment on the
/// function asks for an output buffer of precision plus one bytes, which
/// fits this byte-count reading.
///
/// \param to        [out] Receives up to precision + 1 bytes.
/// \param from      The number.
/// \param precision Digit count of from; read as a byte count (see above).
/// \return Bytes written to to.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This routine, XMP_Encode_Bounded, XMP_Signed_Decode, XMP_Unsigned_Decode
/// and XMP_Randomize treat a digit array as raw bytes with the least
/// significant byte first, so they assume little-endian memory. The byte
/// order of the encoded output is big-endian on any platform.
/// \see XMP_DER_Encode, XMP_Encode_Bounded, XMP_Signed_Decode

/// \fn void XMP_Signed_Decode(digit *result, const unsigned char *from, int frombytes, int precision)
/// \brief Loads a big-endian two's-complement byte string into a number,
///        sign-extended to the number's full width.
///
/// The first byte is the most significant, and its top bit gives the
/// sign. The bytes are stored from the top of result downwards, and the
/// high bytes left over are filled with 0xff for a negative value or 0
/// otherwise. It reverses XMP_Encode and XMP_Encode_Bounded. XMP_DER_Decode
/// calls it after checking the length. int.h also wraps it in Int's
/// Signed_Decode member.
///
/// \param result    [out] The number.
/// \param from      The encoded bytes, most significant first.
/// \param frombytes Number of bytes at from. At least 1 and at most
///                  precision * 4.
/// \param precision Digit count of result.
/// \warning Nothing checks frombytes. If it is above precision * 4, the
///          bytes that do not fit are written into the memory in front of
///          result. XMP_DER_Decode checks the length before calling.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Decode, XMP_DER_Decode, XMP_Encode

/// \fn void XMP_Unsigned_Decode(digit *result, const unsigned char *from, int frombytes, int precision)
/// \brief Loads a big-endian byte string into a number, filling any high
///        bytes left over with 0 whatever the first byte holds.
///
/// The same as XMP_Signed_Decode except that the high bytes left over are
/// always filled with 0. So when frombytes is less than precision * 4, a
/// first byte with its top bit set does not make the number negative. At
/// exactly precision * 4 bytes there is no room for a zero byte: the first
/// byte becomes the top byte of result, and its top bit is the sign bit
/// that XMP_Is_Negative reads. int.h wraps it in Int's Unsigned_Decode
/// member. No retail caller of either is known.
///
/// \param result    [out] The number.
/// \param from      The encoded bytes, most significant first.
/// \param frombytes Number of bytes at from. At least 1 and at most
///                  precision * 4.
/// \param precision Digit count of result.
/// \warning As with XMP_Signed_Decode, a frombytes above precision * 4
///          writes into the memory in front of result.
/// \see XMP_Signed_Decode

/// \fn void XMP_Decode_ASCII(char const *str, digit *mpn, int precision)
/// \brief Parses a number from text that may have a minus sign, commas and
///        a radix suffix.
///
/// The text is an optional leading '-', then digits, then an optional
/// suffix that picks the radix: 'h' for 16, 'o' for 8, 'b' for 2, and '.'
/// or anything else for 10, in either case. The radix comes from the last
/// character of the string before any digit is read. Commas are skipped.
/// Reading stops at the first character that is not a hexadecimal digit,
/// or whose value is not below the radix, which is how the suffix ends it.
/// Each digit multiplies the number by the radix with
/// XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int and adds the digit with XMP_Add_Int, and XMP_Neg
/// applies the minus sign at the end. So "1,000" reads as 1000, "FFh" as
/// 255, "777o" as 511 and "101b" as 5.
///
/// int.h wraps it in Int's static Decode_ASCII member. No retail caller
/// of either is known.
///
/// \param str       The text. Null or empty gives 0.
/// \param mpn       [out] The number. Cleared first.
/// \param precision Digit count of mpn.
/// \note 'b' is also a hexadecimal digit, as the source's own comment
///       warns. A hexadecimal string that ends in 'b' without an 'h' is
///       read as binary and stops at its first digit above 1, so "1Ab"
///       gives 1. A hexadecimal string with no suffix is read as decimal
///       and stops at its first letter.
/// \note Overflow is not reported. The value wraps at precision digits.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int, XMP_Add_Int

/// \fn bool XMP_Is_Small_Prime(const digit *candidate, int precision)
/// \brief Meant to look a small number up in the table of primes up to
///        32719. As written, the lookup compares the wrong value.
///
/// A number with more than one significant digit, or above 32719 (the
/// table's last entry), gets false at once. Otherwise the function runs
/// bsearch over the table with the comparison function pfunc, but passes
/// the address of its candidate pointer rather than the number. pfunc
/// therefore compares the low 16 bits of the number's memory address with
/// the table entries. Assuming the usual 4-byte alignment of digit arrays,
/// such as the register of an Int<64>, those bits are a multiple of 4 and
/// never equal a prime, so in practice the function returns false for
/// every number. XMP_Is_Prime is the only known retail caller. int.h also
/// wraps it in Int's IsSmallPrime member.
///
/// \param candidate The number to look up.
/// \param precision Digit count of candidate.
/// \return true only if the lookup matches, which in practice does not
///         happen (see above).
/// \note Numbers above 32719 never reach the faulty lookup. Generate_Prime
///       draws its q from 2 to the power bits - 2 upwards and tests q and
///       p = 2q + 1, so with a bit count of 17 or more every candidate it
///       tests is above 32719. With a smaller bit count, some candidates
///       can fall in the table's range. Generate_Prime's only known caller,
///       PKey::Generate, passes its own bits argument on and has no known
///       retail caller, so no retail code is known to fix the bit count;
///       see \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath.
/// \see XMP_Is_Prime, XMP_Small_Divisors_Test

/// \fn bool XMP_Small_Divisors_Test(const digit *candidate, int precision)
/// \brief Divides a number by every prime in the 3,511-entry table and
///        fails it as soon as one divides evenly.
///
/// One XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int per table prime, from 2 upwards. Failing
/// shows that a table prime divides the number, so the number is not
/// prime unless it is that table prime itself: every prime up to 32719
/// fails, by dividing itself. Passing proves nothing; it only means there
/// is no factor up to 32719. The number is treated as unsigned.
/// XMP_Is_Prime is the only known retail caller. int.h also wraps it in
/// Int's SmallDivisorsTest member.
///
/// Generate_Prime keeps a RemainderTable over the same table primes for
/// each of its two candidates, q and p, and skips a stepped pair (q + 2,
/// p + 4) when either table shows a zero remainder. The RemainderTable
/// constructor does not mark a zero remainder, so the first pair of each
/// draw is not sieved. A candidate that the sieve lets through has no
/// divisor in the table, so this routine, run by XMP_Is_Prime, then does
/// all 3,511 divisions without finding one.
///
/// \param candidate The number to test.
/// \param precision Digit count of candidate. At most 64: the routine's
///                  scratch quotient holds MAX_UNIT_PRECISION digits.
/// \return false if a table prime divides the number, otherwise true.
/// \see XMP_Is_Prime, XMP_Fetch_Prime_Table, RemainderTable

/// \fn bool XMP_Is_Prime(digit const *prime, int precision)
/// \brief Tests whether a number is probably prime: an even check, a
///        small-prime lookup, trial division, then a Fermat test to bases
///        2 and 3.
///
/// The first step that decides gives the answer:
/// 1. An even number is not prime. This rejects 2 as well.
/// 2. If XMP_Is_Small_Prime finds the number, it is prime.
/// 3. If XMP_Small_Divisors_Test finds a divisor in the table, it is not.
/// 4. If XMP_Fermat_Test fails with two rounds (bases 2 and 3), it is not.
/// 5. Otherwise it is presumed prime.
///
/// XMP_Is_Prime never runs XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test.
///
/// Its known retail callers are Generate_Prime, which calls it through an
/// inlined Int::IsPrime while it searches for a key's primes, and the
/// out-of-line Int::IsPrime of Int<64>. Generate_Prime tests its candidate
/// q first and the matching p only if q passes. It sieves the candidates
/// it steps to with RemainderTable, but the first pair of each draw
/// reaches XMP_Is_Prime unsieved (see XMP_Small_Divisors_Test).
///
/// \param prime     The candidate, treated as unsigned.
/// \param precision Digit count of prime. At most 64, because the tests it
///                  runs keep scratch numbers of MAX_UNIT_PRECISION digits.
/// \return false if the number is proven composite (or is a small prime,
///         see below), true if it is probably prime. A composite number
///         with no factor up to 32719 that passes Fermat's test to bases 2
///         and 3 is accepted.
/// \note Step 2 never succeeds in practice (see XMP_Is_Small_Prime), so the
///       odd primes up to 32719 reach step 3, divide by themselves and are
///       reported composite. Generate_Prime's candidates are at least 2 to
///       the power bits - 2, so with a bit count of 17 or more they are all
///       above 32719 and are not affected. With a smaller bit count, an odd
///       prime candidate in the table's range would in practice be
///       rejected. No retail code is known to fix the bit count (see
///       XMP_Is_Small_Prime).
/// \see XMP_Fermat_Test, XMP_Small_Divisors_Test, Generate_Prime

/// \fn void XMP_Randomize(digit *result, Straw &rng, int total_bits, int precision)
/// \brief Fills a number with total_bits random bits read from a Straw.
///
/// Clears result, reads total_bits / 8 + 1 bytes from rng into its low end
/// with Straw::Get, and masks the last byte down to the total_bits % 8 bits
/// that belong to the number. If the straw's bytes are uniform, so is the
/// result, between 0 and 2 to the power total_bits, minus one. When
/// total_bits is a multiple of 8, the extra byte is masked to 0, so one
/// byte from the straw is read and discarded.
///
/// XMP_Randomize_Bounded is its known retail user; there the body is
/// inlined, and the out-of-line copy has no known direct caller. int.h
/// also wraps it in the Int::Randomize overload that takes a bit count.
///
/// \param result     [out] The random number.
/// \param rng        Source of the bytes. Their quality is the quality of
///                   anything generated from them, such as PKey key pairs.
/// \param total_bits Number of random bits. Reduced to precision * 32 if
///                   larger.
/// \param precision  Digit count of result.
/// \warning At full width, with total_bits at least precision * 32, it
///          reads one byte more than result holds. That byte is stored
///          just past the end of result and then cleared, so the memory
///          after result ends up overwritten with 0.
/// \see XMP_Randomize_Bounded, Straw::Get

/// \fn void XMP_Randomize_Bounded(digit *result, Straw &rng, digit const *minval, digit const *maxval, int precision)
/// \brief Draws a uniformly distributed random number from minval to
///        maxval, both included.
///
/// Rejection sampling: it takes the range maxval - minval, draws random
/// numbers of as many bits as the range has (XMP_Randomize, inlined) until
/// one is not above the range, and adds minval. Each draw is accepted with
/// a probability of at least one half, so few draws are needed. The
/// comparison with the range is unsigned.
///
/// The known retail callers are Generate_Prime, which picks the starting
/// q of each prime search with it through an inlined Int::Randomize; the
/// out-of-line Int::Randomize of Int<64> that takes two bounds, which then
/// sets the lowest bit so its result is odd; and XMP_Rabin_Miller_Test,
/// for its bases.
///
/// \param result    [out] The random number.
/// \param rng       Source of random bytes.
/// \param minval    Smallest value allowed.
/// \param maxval    Largest value allowed. Must not be below minval.
/// \param precision Digit count of the numbers. At most 64: the range is
///                  kept in a scratch number of MAX_UNIT_PRECISION digits.
/// \see XMP_Randomize, Generate_Prime
